  "explanation": {
    "short": "Every letter has a short \"a\" inside it, but at the end of a word that \"a\" is not said: नाम is nām (not \"nāma\"), घर is ghar, चाय is cāy. A vowel at the very start of a word is written with its own letter: आ is the long \"aa\" of आप and आज, and औ is the \"au\" of और.",
    "more": "The last \"a\" at the end of a word falls silent. In the middle of a short word the short \"a\" is usually said (नमक is na-mak), but in longer words a middle \"a\" is often dropped too (you will meet this later, with लड़का). Hindi never pronounces a letter you cannot hear in everyday speech: you learn which \"a\" to drop by hearing words."
  },
